WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:03.320
You may not think of spiders as the maternal sort.

00:00:03.320 --> 00:00:04.920
But some do make good moms.

00:00:05.340 --> 00:00:07.860
Wolf spiders carry their egg sacs around.

00:00:08.060 --> 00:00:11.740
And after hatching, the spiderlings ride on mom’s back.

00:00:11.740 --> 00:00:14.720
This ant-mimicking jumping spider takes another tack—

00:00:14.720 --> 00:00:17.810
she provides milk to her babies.

00:00:17.810 --> 00:00:19.980
At first, they suck up little droplets that

00:00:19.980 --> 00:00:22.160
the mother places around the nest.

00:00:22.160 --> 00:00:24.140
As they get larger, the spiderlings suckle

00:00:24.140 --> 00:00:27.280
straight from their mom’s epigastric furrow.

00:00:28.560 --> 00:00:31.820
In lab tests, researchers found they continued to suckle

00:00:31.820 --> 00:00:34.540
until the spiders became subadults—

00:00:34.540 --> 00:00:37.220
which is about 40 days after hatching.

00:00:37.230 --> 00:00:38.760
Researchers were able to figure this out

00:00:38.760 --> 00:00:42.290
by carefully watching these families in their nests.

00:00:42.290 --> 00:00:43.720
Even though the mother spider never brought

00:00:43.720 --> 00:00:46.649
anything back to the nest to eat, the spiderlings

00:00:46.649 --> 00:00:49.690
tripled in length in the first 20 days.

00:00:49.690 --> 00:00:51.420
To look more closely at the benefit of this

00:00:51.420 --> 00:00:54.320
milk and maternal care, the researchers painted

00:00:54.320 --> 00:00:56.180
over the mom’s epigastric furrow

00:00:56.180 --> 00:00:58.820
to cut off the milk supply.

00:00:58.820 --> 00:01:01.020
Without milk, they found that the young didn’t grow

00:01:01.020 --> 00:01:03.100
and died about 10 days later.

00:01:03.810 --> 00:01:05.960
When they removed the mother after 20 days,

00:01:05.960 --> 00:01:08.280
the subadults foraged on their own--

00:01:08.280 --> 00:01:10.320
more than they would if mom were there but

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:12.720
fewer survived over all.

00:01:12.960 --> 00:01:15.080
What is spider milk anyway?

00:01:15.640 --> 00:01:18.420
The milk comes out of this egg-laying opening

00:01:18.420 --> 00:01:20.320
the spiders have--so one idea is that the

00:01:20.320 --> 00:01:22.960
spider milk might be evolutionarily

00:01:22.960 --> 00:01:25.259
related to trophic eggs.

00:01:25.259 --> 00:01:27.580
This is when animals feed infertile eggs

00:01:27.580 --> 00:01:29.360
to their offspring.

00:01:29.360 --> 00:01:31.440
Spider milk is protein rich.

00:01:31.440 --> 00:01:33.740
An analysis of the milk droplets revealed

00:01:33.740 --> 00:01:36.300
the protein content was around 4 times

00:01:36.300 --> 00:01:38.130
that of cow’s milk

00:01:38.130 --> 00:01:41.540
While it is not usual for animals, even invertebrates

00:01:41.540 --> 00:01:44.189
like spiders and cockroaches, to give their

00:01:44.189 --> 00:01:46.640
food to their young--the length and intensity

00:01:46.640 --> 00:01:48.970
of feeding and maternal care given by

00:01:48.970 --> 00:01:52.240
these jumping spider moms was thought to only happen

00:01:52.240 --> 00:01:55.560
in cognitively advanced, social vertebrates,

00:01:55.560 --> 00:01:59.310
like orangutans, people, and and elephants.

00:01:59.310 --> 00:02:01.040
Researchers speculate that if there’s a
chance

00:02:01.040 --> 00:02:03.390
there will be no food for newborns,

00:02:03.390 --> 00:02:06.360
or all the young spiders are very likely to get

00:02:06.360 --> 00:02:08.759
eaten by predators, then it can make sense

00:02:08.759 --> 00:02:12.310
for a mother to take on the cost of parental care.

00:02:12.310 --> 00:02:14.300
This milk-giving spider suggests scientists

00:02:14.300 --> 00:02:16.760
will need to rethink the extent of maternal

00:02:16.760 --> 00:02:19.180
care and milk provisioning

00:02:19.180 --> 00:02:20.810
throughout the animal kingdom

00:02:20.810 --> 00:02:22.720
And how much cognitive power you need

00:02:22.720 --> 00:02:25.090
to take care of offspring in these ways.

